  "summary": "Lace-carved crosses blooming from stone — 40,000 memorial stelae no two alike.",
  "description": "Khachkars — standing memorial stones whose central cross erupts into rosette, knot and solar-disk lacework — are Armenia's signature sculptural form, with some 40,000 surviving examples recorded. Master carvers (varpet) signed works and competed in the fineness of their ornament: the late-13th-century carvings of Momik at Noravank pushed the style to engraving-like delicacy, while field khachkars mark graves, vows, victories and church consecrations. UNESCO inscribed the craft in 2010; the destruction of Julfa's medieval khachkar cemetery in Nakhichevan made them also a symbol of heritage under threat.",
  "culturalContext": "Khachkars mark every Armenian life event — graves, vows, victories, church consecrations — and encode national memory: Momik's masterworks are signed art history, Julfa's destroyed cemetery made them heritage-under-threat symbols, and UNESCO's 2010 listing recognized a craft central to Armenian identity across the diaspora.",
